### Is this a regression?

No

### Description

When OS-level motion is allowed (no reduced-motion preference) and the app sets global CSS:

```
html {
  scroll-behavior: smooth;
}
```

Angular’s ViewportScroller scrolls to a target and then focuses it for accessibility, specifically in the scrollToAnchor method.

```
 scrollToAnchor(target: string, options?: ScrollOptions): void {
    const elSelected = findAnchorFromDocument(this.document, target);

    if (elSelected) {
      this.scrollToElement(elSelected, options);
      // After scrolling to the element, the spec dictates that we follow the focus steps for the
      // target. Rather than following the robust steps, simply attempt focus.
      //
      // @see https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#get-the-focusable-area
      // @see https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/HTMLOrForeignElement/focus
      // @see https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#focusable-area
      elSelected.focus();
    }
  }
```

Because smooth scrolling is asynchronous, Angular’s assumption that window scrolling completes synchronously no longer holds. The subsequent focus() call can interrupt the in-progress animation, resulting in an incorrect final scroll position (the viewport ends up offset incorrectly compared to the intended anchor position).

This behavior is partially addressed in Angular 20.1, which introduces an options parameter for all ViewportScroller methods so scroll behavior can be explicitly configured and overridden. However, when behavior: 'smooth' is used (or when smooth behavior is still desired), the same glitch occurs: the focus call competes with the in-progress smooth scroll.

